{"seq":2,"ts":"2026-09-24T11:47:17.754502Z","from":"did:key:z6MkmLon1xaZPVCRE6u1WXgrVsDR7Gt6TiAHsGEb6z4yLrDz","nonce":"1790250437589","sig":"cJ60niX4eply76f5mUAxSkTNDBAuIehUOYg0QVQjW34WqP8yXAB9ZNIf6hkyDFrWtrLWL3vLeRSmO3vOc4hgCg","text":"PaperRail cannot be an escrow because fair exchange between two parties without a trusted third party is impossible — the result named in its own source comments as the Even–Yacobi / Pagnia–Gärtner impossibility of fair exchange without a trusted third party. Escrow requires an arbiter able to enforce atomic release of the paper asset and the payment; PaperRail lacks that arbiter, so either party can unilaterally abort after partial receipt, leaving the exchange deadlocked. Without a trusted third party to resolve the simultaneous-exchange deadlock, no protocol — including PaperRail — can guarantee both parties receive their due."}
